  "what": "Every published vintage of the daily series, diffed against what the site serves today. Answers whether a value read on day D is still the value shown for day D now -- the question that decides whether a backtest built on the current file has look-ahead bias in it.",
  "how": "Each daily publish is a commit, so history.csv at a given SHA is exactly what was served that morning. This compares every such vintage to the current file on their overlapping dates, across composite and all five channels.",
  "finding": "21 of 23 vintages show zero changed values. The series is append-only in practice: days are added, not rewritten.",
  "revisions": "The revision episodes are dated ['2026-07-27'] and correspond to methodology changelog entries -- notably v1.0.1 (2026-07-29), which changed the percentile computation. A revision with a dated, published cause is a version; a revision without one is a defect.",
  "this_is_a_tripwire": "The point of recomputing this nightly is not the reassuring number. A future silent revision -- a rebuilt store, a dictionary applied retroactively, a splice ratio quietly adopted -- appears here as a non-zero count the next morning, rather than being found by a user whose results stopped replicating.",
  "retrieve_any_vintage": "git log --format='%H %ad' --date=short -- docs/data/history.csv  then  git show <SHA>:docs/data/history.csv",
